QB.add(71,[{"n":1,"q":"A 55-year-old male with a history of liver cirrhosis presents to the emergency department with hematemesis and signs of shock. He is diagnosed with acute variceal bleeding. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management step for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Administer intravenous terlipressin followed by prophylactic intravenous antibiotics"},{"t":"Start intravenous antibiotics and perform urgent endoscopy for band ligation"},{"t":"Administer intravenous vasopressin and initiate fresh frozen plasma transfusion"},{"t":"Begin intravenous corticosteroids and monitor for signs of hepatic encephalopathy"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Administer intravenous terlipressin followed by prophylactic intravenous antibiotics"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In acute variceal bleeding, the initial management should include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Terlipressin : This vasopressin analogue reduces portal hypertension by causing splanchnic vasoconstriction, effectively controlling bleeding from varices. Other vasoactive drugs used in controlling variceal bleeding are Octreotide and Somatostatin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other vasoactive drugs used in controlling variceal bleeding are Octreotide and Somatostatin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prophylactic Antibiotics : Administering antibiotics helps prevent infections like spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP), which can complicate variceal bleeding."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other vasoactive drugs used in controlling variceal bleeding are Octreotide and Somatostatin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of non-bleeding esophageal varices"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endoscopic variceal ligation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta blockers (propanolol, nadolol)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management for bleeding esophageal varices"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute variceal bleeding approach"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recurrent variceal bleeding approach"}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 50-year-old male with a history of alcohol abuse and long-term hypertension presents with hematemesis, abdominal distension, and splenomegaly. He is hypotensive and tachycardic, with elevated liver enzymes, low platelets, and a high INR. Endoscopy reveals esophageal varices. Which of the following most likely explains the development of this condition?","o":[{"t":"Portal venous thrombosis leads to increased collateral circulation"},{"t":"Increased portal blood flow to the liver"},{"t":"Increased portal pressure forcing blood into collateral vessels"},{"t":"Reduced portal vascular resistance"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: C) Increased portal pressure forcing blood into collateral vessels"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient appears to have cirrhosis resulting from chronic alcohol use, complicated by the development of esophageal varices."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The pathophysiology of esophageal varices in this context is mainly due to portal hypertension associated with cirrhosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology of esophageal varices in case of cirrhosis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endoscopy is the most definitive method for diagnosing esophageal varices."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ultrasound and CT/MRI imaging help assess the liver and portal circulation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Portal pressure management to measure wedged hepatic venous pressure (WHVP) by balloon catheter through transjugular route."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Portal venous thrombosis leading to increased collateral circulation (Option A) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Portal venous thrombosis (PVT) can cause portal hypertension, but in this patient with cirrhosis, the primary cause is liver fibrosis and scarring, leading to increased portal pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PVT is less common in this context compared to cirrhosis."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 62-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis presents to the emergency department with confusion, disorientation, and asterixis. His family reports increasing drowsiness and difficulty concentrating over the past few days. Investigations reveal normal electrolytes and elevated ammonia levels. What is the most appropriate initial treatment to lower ammonia?","o":[{"t":"Increase the protein intake"},{"t":"Zinc supplementation"},{"t":"Rifaximin"},{"t":"Lactulose"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Lactulose"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this clinical scenario, the patient is likely experiencing hepatic encephalopathy due to elevated ammonia levels associated with alcoholic cirrhosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most appropriate initial treatment to lower ammonia is Lactulose. It is a nonabsorbable disaccharide that acidifies the colon , converting ammonia (NH3) into ammonium (NH4+). It aids in removing nitrogenous products from the gut that contribute to encephalopathy and acts as a laxative."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a nonabsorbable disaccharide that acidifies the colon , converting ammonia (NH3) into ammonium (NH4+)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It aids in removing nitrogenous products from the gut that contribute to encephalopathy and acts as a laxative."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a nonabsorbable disaccharide that acidifies the colon , converting ammonia (NH3) into ammonium (NH4+)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It aids in removing nitrogenous products from the gut that contribute to encephalopathy and acts as a laxative."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hepatic Encephalopathy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Etiopathogenesis It is a complication of chronic liver disease that leads to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"impaired brain function as a result of liver failure. It is also called as portosystemic encephalopathy. Ammonia is a gut-derived neurotoxin that is not detoxified into urea and bypasses the liver due to portosystemic shunting and decreased hepatic mass , allowing it to enter the bloodstream and impact the brain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types Type A is caused by acute liver failure & cerebral"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"oedema is a common side effect of this type."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type B is caused by portosystemic shunting (can be due to TIPS also )"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type C is due to chronic liver failure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical manifestations Grade 0 - alterations are seen in neuropsychological"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"tests, but only minimal symptomatic changes are evident in Short-term memory, Reaction time or Concentration."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Grade 1: Mood changes like anxiety, euphoria; Memory trouble, Trivial lack of awareness, Difficulty with addition and subtraction, Impaired sleep pattern."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Grade 2: Personality changes Lethargy & apathy Behavior changes Dysarthria Disorientation for time"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Grade 3: Severe confusion, delirium Disorientation to place Drowsiness Bizarre behavior Asterixis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Grade 4: Coma (total loss of consciousness)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Grade 0,1 are considered convert or minimal hepatic encephalopathy, and grades 2,3,4 are overt hepatic encephalopathy with evident symptoms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations Psychometric tests"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Complete clinical evaluation Blood investigations like LFT Doppler ultrasound to evaluate the liver EEG & MRI to assess brain activity"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management Lactulose"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Rifaximin (Option C) reduces ammonia production; it is an adjunctive therapy in patients who do not tolerate lactulose. Neomycin is an alternative to Rifaximin. Branched-chain amino acids and probiotics Zinc supplementation , used as adjunct therapy (Option B) Restricted protein intak e was used in the past but is not commonly used due to its risk of malnutrition outweighs the benefits (Option A) IV hydration is a vital supportive measure"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 60-year-old woman with decompensated cirrhosis due to hepatitis B presents for evaluation of liver transplantation. All the following are indications for liver transplantation except?","o":[{"t":"Autoimmune hepatitis"},{"t":"Caroli’s disease"},{"t":"Fulminant hepatitis"},{"t":"Portal vein thrombosis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Portal vein thrombosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Portal vein thrombosis is considered a relative contraindication for liver transplantation as it can affect the viability of the donor's liver and complicate the transplantation process by disrupting the normal blood flow to the liver."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is not an absolute contraindication because portal vein thrombosis can be managed by grafting the donor's portal vein to the recipient's superior mesenteric vein , ensuring adequate venous drainage. Contraindications for Liver Transplantation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Absolute Relative"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Active sepsis Elderly age (> 70) Uncontrolled extra hepatobiliary infection History of extensive hepatobiliary Life-limiting congenital anomalies surgery Substance abuse Renal failure, which is not due to liver Severe cardiopulmonary condition disease Extra Hepatobiliary malignancy (excluding Portal vein thrombosis non-melanoma malignancy skin cancer) Severe obesity or malnutrition, Metastasis of Liver Cancer History of extrahepatic cancer Cholangiocarcinoma (excluding tumours fitting into the Non-compliant with medicines protocol) Seropositive HIV with CD4 <100/mL AIDS Intrahepatic sepsis Severe systemic disorders Increased pulmonary HTN"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Uncontrolled psychiatric disorder"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indications of liver transplantation in adults: Autoimmune hepatitis (Option A) Biliary Cirrhosis Caroli’s disease (Option B) Cryptogenic cirrhosis Hepatic vein thrombosis Fulminant hepatitis (Option C) Alcoholic cirrhosis Chronic viral hepatitis Primary hepatocellular malignancies Hepatic adenomas NASH Familial amyloid polyneuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune hepatitis (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biliary Cirrhosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Caroli’s disease (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cryptogenic cirrhosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic vein thrombosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fulminant hepatitis (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcoholic cirrhosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic viral hepatitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary hepatocellular malignancies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic adenomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NASH"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Familial amyloid polyneuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HLA matching is not required as the presence of HLA antibodies does not exclude a patient from receiving a transplant."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MELD score is used to prioritise patients for liver transplantation based on the severity of their liver disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Components of UW (University of Wisconsin) solution used for cold ischemia is Lactobionate Rannose"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lactobionate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rannose"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pH of the UW solution is around 7.4 at 20°C, with an osmolarity of 320 mmol/kg. The temperature is reduced to 2-6°C prior to its use."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cold ischemic time is up to 20 hours , but the ideal time limit is 12 hours ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune hepatitis (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biliary Cirrhosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Caroli’s disease (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cryptogenic cirrhosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic vein thrombosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fulminant hepatitis (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcoholic cirrhosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic viral hepatitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary hepatocellular malignancies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic adenomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NASH"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Familial amyloid polyneuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lactobionate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rannose"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lobe preferred in donor for harvesting."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"To an adult recipient Right lobe"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"To a child recipient Left lobe or a part of it."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 62-year-old male with a history of cirrhosis due to chronic hepatitis C presents with worsening abdominal distension, jaundice, and decreased urine output over the past week. He exhibits signs of confusion and ascites resistant to diuretics. Laboratory tests reveal a serum creatinine level of 2.5 mg/dL. Given this presentation, which of the following is the most definitive management of this condition?","o":[{"t":"Hemodialysis"},{"t":"Liver transplantation"},{"t":"Vasoconstrictor therapy with Terlipressin"},{"t":"Albumin infusion"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Liver transplantation"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's condition is hepatorenal syndrome (HRS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While it is initially managed with albumin infusion and vasoconstrictors (such as terlipressin, octreotide, or midodrine), liver transplantation remains the most definitive and effective treatment for HRS."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hepatorenal Syndrome (HRS)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Description A functional renal failure resulting from advanced"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cirrhosis or acute liver failure without any underlying renal pathology."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mechanism Vasodilatory prostaglandins ( PGE2 and PGI2 )"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"enhance renal blood flow and increase glomerular filtration rate to maintain perfusion, while in hepatosplenomegaly: The cirrhotic liver produces endothelin-1 , resulting in kidney injury, as:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criteria Refractory ascites (predispose to HRS)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Serum creatinine >1.5 mg% (progressive rise) Stop diuretics (causes dehydration) Volume expansion (volume given along with albumin will increase urine output) No shock (but vitals monitoring is required) No nephrotoxic drug exposure No history of parenchymal disease (ruled out by imaging like ultrasound)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Types Type I HRS or HRS-Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Progressive oliguria (renal function impairment) (Option B) Rapid rise of the serum creatinine Very poor prognosis Type II HRS or HRS Non-Acute Kidney Injury (NAKI) Associated with refractory ascites Gradual increase in serum creatinine (moderate and stable) Better prognosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features History of chronic liver disease"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Ascites (refractory or tense). Respiratory distress (shortness of breath) Variceal bleeding (hematemesis) Deranged vitals Decreased urine output or anuria"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Definitive treatment: Orthoptic liver transplant (OLT)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pharmacological treatment: Following drugs can be used Albumin infusion with vasoconstrictors (terlipressin, octreotide, midodrine) (Option C & D) Norepinephrine can also be used. Hemodialysis is not used mostly as it does not improve the outcome of the condition (Option A)"}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 54-year-old female with diabetes, obesity, and recent laparoscopic surgery presents to the emergency department with high fever, chills, rapid heart rate, extreme fatigue, and worsening abdominal pain. The physician suspects Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (SIRS) and decides to perform an investigation as part of the diagnostic criteria. Which investigation should be performed?","o":[{"t":"Blood culture"},{"t":"Urine culture"},{"t":"Urine routine microscopy"},{"t":"Complete blood count"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Complete blood count."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A Complete Blood Count (CBC) is essential in evaluating Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (SIRS) as it helps identify leukocytosis (elevated white blood cell count), which is one of the diagnostic criteria for SIRS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"SIRS (Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A sepsis-like condition associated with systemic inflammation that can be triggered by various non-microbial factors (burns, trauma, infections)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Criteria for diagnosis: 2 or more of the following components should be present to confirm the diagnosis of SIRS. Component Range"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Temperature (fever) Hyperthermia (fever) >38°C (>100.4°F) or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hypothermia <36°C (<96.8°F)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Leukocyte (white blood cell) Leukocytosis >12,000/mL count Leukopenia <4000/mL"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Respiratory rate or ABG Tachypnea (>20 breaths per minute) or partial pressure showing of CO2 less than 32 mmHg, respectively."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Heart rate Tachycardia (>90 breaths per minute)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blood culture (Option A) is performed to check for sepsis. If SIRS is left untreated, it can progress to sepsis, indicated by a positive blood culture in addition to SIRS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Urine culture (Option B) and Urine routine microscopy (Option C) can also be helpful in management, but they are not included in the criteria for SIRS."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 62-year-old male with malnutrition and alcoholic liver disease presents with increasing abdominal distension, shortness of breath, fever, and significant weight loss. The ascitic fluid shows a neutrophil count greater than 250/mL. Which of the following statements about this condition are true? 1. Ascitic fluid appears transparent 2. Most commonly caused by Escherichia coli 3. Cefotaxime can be used for treatment 4. Norfloxacin administration may help to prevent it","o":[{"t":"1, 2, 3"},{"t":"1, 2, 4"},{"t":"2, 3, 4"},{"t":"1, 2, 3, 4"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) 2, 3, 4"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, a malnourished patient with a history of alcoholic liver disease presents with ascites, fever, and ascitic fluid showing a neutrophil count greater than 250/mL, indicating a diagnosis of Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP) , with the ascitic fluid appearing turbid due to pus."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Description A severe complication of ascites, characterised by"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"spontaneous infection of the ascitic fluid without an intra-abdominal source."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Causative Escherichia coli (most common) and other gut bacteria organism (Statement 2)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Gram-positive bacteria ( Streptococcus viridans, Staphylococcus aureus , and Enterococcus )"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Source of infecti on"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Worsening of ascites"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Fever, nausea, vomiting Increase in abdominal girth Encephalopathy"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnosis Ascitic fluid analysis shows:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Turbid appearance (Statement 1) Turbid ascitic fluid\" data-author=\"Piña-Pedraza, J.P. & Álvarez-Avalos, L & Vargas-Espinosa, J.M. & Salcedo-Gómez, A & Carranza-Madrigal, Jaime\" data-hash=\"10419\" data-license=\"CC BY NC ND 4.0\" data-source=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/A-Asci tic-fluid-with-a-turbid-aspect-obtained-from-the-first-parac entesis-B-Ascitic_fig4_281194792\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com /notes/h25bGkoT98SgRGRpL82m1744764985.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Neutrophil count >250/mL Culture and gram satins reveal the causative organisms"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management IV 3rd generation cephalosporins (cefotaxime) for 5"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"days (Statement 3)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prevention Paracentesis within 12 hours of hospital admission"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Norfloxacin administration (cirrhotic patients with an episode of SBP) (Statement 4) Quinolone antibiotic prophylaxis (recurrent episodes of SBP in variceal bleeding)."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 70-year-old male with a history of congestive heart failure, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease is scheduled for a procedure as depicted in the image below. What is the name of the procedure?","o":[{"t":"Pericardiocentesis"},{"t":"Thoracentesis"},{"t":"Paracentesis"},{"t":"Diagnostic peritoneal lavage"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Paracentesis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, the patient with a history of congestive heart failure, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease is undergoing Paracentesis (the removal of fluid from the abdominal cavity), as shown in the image."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a bedside procedure in which a needle or small catheter is inserted transcutaneously to extract ascitic fluid from the peritoneal cavity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Site: Left lower quadrant"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Procedure: The needle is inserted (USG guided) at a 45-degree angle using the Z-track technique (minimise leakage following the procedure)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The needle is inserted (USG guided) at a 45-degree angle using the Z-track technique (minimise leakage following the procedure)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The needle is inserted (USG guided) at a 45-degree angle using the Z-track technique (minimise leakage following the procedure)."},{"k":"img","v":9351},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advance the needle slowly through the abdominal wall until you feel a pop or loss of resistance, indicating entry into the peritoneal cavity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Both diagnostic (microscopic examination, cytology, gram stain, culture, and sugar and protein values) and therapeutic aspiration were done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Interpretation: Secondary peritonitis: Low sugar values, high LDH values Pancreatic aetiology: Raised amylase Biliary tract perforation: Dark brown fluid"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary peritonitis: Low sugar values, high LDH values"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancreatic aetiology: Raised amylase"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biliary tract perforation: Dark brown fluid"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary peritonitis: Low sugar values, high LDH values"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancreatic aetiology: Raised amylase"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biliary tract perforation: Dark brown fluid"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pericardiocentesis (Option A) is a medical procedure that involves inserting a needle into the pericardial cavity for therapeutic and diagnostic purposes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is typically performed in conditions like cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"img","v":9357},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thoracentesis (Option B) is a medical procedure that involves inserting a needle or catheter into the pleural space of the chest to remove fluid or air for diagnostic or therapeutic reasons."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is typically performed in cases of pleural effusion, which can be caused by conditions such as lung carcinoma, cirrhosis, or tuberculosis."},{"k":"img","v":9362},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic peritoneal lavage (Option D) is used to assess intra-abdominal bleeding, typically in trauma cases, not for chronic fluid accumulation like ascites."}],"qi":[9321]},{"n":9,"q":"A 58-year-old female with chronic alcoholism, hepatitis C, and hypertension presents with abdominal distension, shortness of breath while lying flat, and fatigue. Ultrasound shows splenomegaly and an increased amount of peritoneal fluid. What are the expected serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) and ascitic protein levels in this case?","o":[{"t":"<1.1 g/dL and <2.5 g/dL"},{"t":"‡1.1 g/dL and <2.5 g/dL"},{"t":"<1.1 g/dL and ‡2.5 g/dL"},{"t":"‡1.1 g/dL and ‡2.5 g/dL"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) ‡1.1 g/dL and <2.5 g/dL"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient likely has ascites due to portal hypertension , commonly associated with cirrhosis from chronic alcoholism and hepatitis C."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In such cases, the serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG ) is typically ‡1.1 g/dL, indicating portal hypertension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ascitic protein level is usually <2.5 g/dL, reflecting low protein transudative fluid associated with cirrhosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The SAAG reflects hepatic sinusoidal pressure and correlates with the hepatic venous pressure gradient, calculated by subtracting the ascitic albumin from the serum albumin (there is no effect of diuresis)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Interpretations: SAAG ‡1.1 g/dL: Portal hypertension (ascites is due to increased pressure in the hepatic sinusoids) Ascitic protein level ‡2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are normal (allows passage of protein) Ascitic protein level <2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are damaged and scarred (no passage of protein) SAAG <1.1 g/dL: Ascites is not related to portal hypertension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SAAG ‡1.1 g/dL: Portal hypertension (ascites is due to increased pressure in the hepatic sinusoids) Ascitic protein level ‡2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are normal (allows passage of protein) Ascitic protein level <2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are damaged and scarred (no passage of protein)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascitic protein level ‡2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are normal (allows passage of protein)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascitic protein level <2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are damaged and scarred (no passage of protein)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SAAG <1.1 g/dL: Ascites is not related to portal hypertension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SAAG ‡1.1 g/dL: Portal hypertension (ascites is due to increased pressure in the hepatic sinusoids) Ascitic protein level ‡2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are normal (allows passage of protein) Ascitic protein level <2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are damaged and scarred (no passage of protein)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascitic protein level ‡2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are normal (allows passage of protein)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascitic protein level <2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are damaged and scarred (no passage of protein)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SAAG <1.1 g/dL: Ascites is not related to portal hypertension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascitic protein level ‡2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are normal (allows passage of protein)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascitic protein level <2.5 g/dL : Hepatic sinusoids are damaged and scarred (no passage of protein)"}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 62-year-old male with chronic liver disease, hepatitis C, and diabetes presents to the emergency department with worsening abdominal distension, shortness of breath, and fatigue. His swelling has worsened despite cutting sodium intake and increasing diuretics, and a fluid thrill is noted on examination.\nWhich of the following medications is contraindicated in this case?","o":[{"t":"Clonidine"},{"t":"Amiloride"},{"t":"Midodrine"},{"t":"Propranolol"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Propranolol"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The scenario describes refractory ascites that is unresponsive to sodium restriction and increased diuretics."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Propranolol , a non-selective beta-blocker, is contraindicated as it can reduce cardiac output and worsen outcomes by negatively affecting hemodynamics."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of Ascites"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mild ascites : Sodium restriction in diet"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Moderate ascites Sodium restriction in diet Diuretics: Spironolactone (maximum dose: 400mg) Furosemide (maximum dose 160mg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sodium restriction in diet"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diuretics: Spironolactone (maximum dose: 400mg) Furosemide (maximum dose 160mg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spironolactone (maximum dose: 400mg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Furosemide (maximum dose 160mg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Refractory ascites (A significant amount of ascites, despite adherence to a low-sodium diet and diuretic therapy) Amiloride (diuretic) (Option B) Midodrine (alpha-1 agonist, vasoconstrictor) (Option C) : Vasoconstriction of splanchnic artery Clonidine (alpha-2 agonist, vasoconstrictor) (Option A) Beta-blockers are contraindicated in refractory ascites (increase mortality) (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amiloride (diuretic) (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Midodrine (alpha-1 agonist, vasoconstrictor) (Option C) : Vasoconstriction of splanchnic artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clonidine (alpha-2 agonist, vasoconstrictor) (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta-blockers are contraindicated in refractory ascites (increase mortality) (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sodium restriction in diet"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diuretics: Spironolactone (maximum dose: 400mg) Furosemide (maximum dose 160mg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spironolactone (maximum dose: 400mg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Furosemide (maximum dose 160mg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spironolactone (maximum dose: 400mg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Furosemide (maximum dose 160mg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amiloride (diuretic) (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Midodrine (alpha-1 agonist, vasoconstrictor) (Option C) : Vasoconstriction of splanchnic artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clonidine (alpha-2 agonist, vasoconstrictor) (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta-blockers are contraindicated in refractory ascites (increase mortality) (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adverse effects: Spironolactone: Gynaecomastia (painful) TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt): Hepatic encephalopathy. LVP (large volume paracentesis): Asterixis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spironolactone: Gynaecomastia (painful)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt): Hepatic encephalopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"LVP (large volume paracentesis): Asterixis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spironolactone: Gynaecomastia (painful)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt): Hepatic encephalopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"LVP (large volume paracentesis): Asterixis"}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 58-year-old man with chronic hepatitis and alcohol use presents with abdominal distension, discomfort, shortness of breath, and a weight gain of 5 kg over two weeks. Physical examination reveals thrombocytopenia, splenomegaly, and ascites. With approximately 120 mL of peritoneal fluid, which physical examination sign is most likely to be observed?","o":[{"t":"Fluid thrill"},{"t":"Shifting dullness"},{"t":"Puddle sign"},{"t":"All of the above"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Puddle sign"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"With small volumes of ascitic fluid (120 mL), the puddle sign is the most detectable indicator, assessed in the sitting position."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The fluid thrill and shifting dullness require larger volumes to be reliably observed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thus, the puddle sign is the most significant finding in this case."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascites is defined as an accumulation of free fluid in the peritoneal cavity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal peritoneal fluid is nil. For females, peritoneal fluid can be 10-20 ml during the mid-ovulatory cycle."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For females, peritoneal fluid can be 10-20 ml during the mid-ovulatory cycle."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For females, peritoneal fluid can be 10-20 ml during the mid-ovulatory cycle."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical manifestations"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initially asymptomatic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Approximately 1-2 litres of fluid show signs and symptoms, including: Abdominal distension, discomfort, pain. Weight gain (sudden) Nausea, vomiting Respiratory distress having shortness of breath, dyspnea Edema of body parts Everted umbilicus (due to an increase in ascitic fluid)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abdominal distension, discomfort, pain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight gain (sudden)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nausea, vomiting"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory distress having shortness of breath, dyspnea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Edema of body parts"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Everted umbilicus (due to an increase in ascitic fluid)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abdominal distension, discomfort, pain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight gain (sudden)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nausea, vomiting"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory distress having shortness of breath, dyspnea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Edema of body parts"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Everted umbilicus (due to an increase in ascitic fluid)"},{"k":"img","v":9371},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical signs:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Puddle sign (Peritoneal fluid >100ml) (Option C) The puddle sign detects small amounts of fluid in the abdomen. In a sitting position, tapping the abdomen produces a distinct sound over fluid, indicating ascites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The puddle sign detects small amounts of fluid in the abdomen."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a sitting position, tapping the abdomen produces a distinct sound over fluid, indicating ascites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The puddle sign detects small amounts of fluid in the abdomen."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a sitting position, tapping the abdomen produces a distinct sound over fluid, indicating ascites."},{"k":"img","v":9374},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shifting dullness (peritoneal fluid >500ml) (Option B) In a supine position, a healthcare provider percusses the abdomen to identify dullness (fluid) and resonance (air). When the patient turns to one side, the fluid shifts, causing dullness to move to the dependent side and resonance to appear on the upper side, indicating ascitic fluid."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a supine position, a healthcare provider percusses the abdomen to identify dullness (fluid) and resonance (air)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When the patient turns to one side, the fluid shifts, causing dullness to move to the dependent side and resonance to appear on the upper side, indicating ascitic fluid."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a supine position, a healthcare provider percusses the abdomen to identify dullness (fluid) and resonance (air)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When the patient turns to one side, the fluid shifts, causing dullness to move to the dependent side and resonance to appear on the upper side, indicating ascitic fluid."},{"k":"img","v":9376},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid thrill or fluid wave test (Option A) The fluid thrill is a sign of large abdominal fluid volumes (more than 1.5 Liters). It occurs when a provider taps one side of the abdomen while feeling the opposite, creating a wave indicating ascites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The fluid thrill is a sign of large abdominal fluid volumes (more than 1.5 Liters)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It occurs when a provider taps one side of the abdomen while feeling the opposite, creating a wave indicating ascites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The fluid thrill is a sign of large abdominal fluid volumes (more than 1.5 Liters)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It occurs when a provider taps one side of the abdomen while feeling the opposite, creating a wave indicating ascites."},{"k":"img","v":9383},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic hydrothorax Fluid from the abdomen, usually due to cirrhosis, accumulates in the pleural cavity, typically on the right side, causing shortness of breath and chest discomfort."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid from the abdomen, usually due to cirrhosis, accumulates in the pleural cavity, typically on the right side, causing shortness of breath and chest discomfort."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid from the abdomen, usually due to cirrhosis, accumulates in the pleural cavity, typically on the right side, causing shortness of breath and chest discomfort."},{"k":"img","v":9389},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abdominal compartment syndrome (intra-abdominal hypertension) can occur if the pressure due to ascites is more than 25 mm Hg."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 52-year-old male presents with progressive fatigue, abdominal distension, and worsening jaundice over the past three months. Examination reveals ascites, spider angiomata, and palmar erythema. Laboratory tests show elevated liver enzymes with a significantly elevated AST/ALT ratio, and an ultrasound indicates a nodular liver. What role do kupffer cells play in the pathogenesis of this condition?","o":[{"t":"Activate CYP2E1"},{"t":"Increase the fatty acid uptake"},{"t":"Convert acetaldehyde to acetate"},{"t":"Induce cytokine production"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Induce cytokine production."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation suggests alcoholic cirrhosis , indicated by symptoms, an elevated AST/ALT ratio, and nodular liver on ultrasound."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kupffer cells , activated by alcohol metabolites and reactive oxygen species, release pro-inflammatory cytokines (e.g., TNF-a, interleukins), which stimulate hepatic stellate cells, leading to fibrosis and micronodular cirrhosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALD presents as micronodular cirrhosis in the early stages, which may progress to a mixed pattern of micronodular and macronodular cirrhosis in the later stages."},{"k":"img","v":9395},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathogenesis of Alcoholic Cirrhosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Enzymes that help in metabolising alcohol in the liver : Cytosolic ADH, Microsomal ethanol oxidising system (MEOS) Peroxisomal catalase"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cytosolic ADH,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Microsomal ethanol oxidising system (MEOS)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peroxisomal catalase"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcohol is metabolised to acetaldehyde by cytosolic alcohol dehydrogenase , which will cause reactive oxygen species to contribute to liver cell damage. (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In chronic alcohol consumption, There is reduced fatty acid oxidation and lipoprotein secretion, leading to increased fatty acid uptake , which causes intracellular accumulation of triglycerides. (Option B) The MEOS pathway activates CYP2E1, resulting in increased production of reactive oxygen species and more acetaldehyde, which is converted to acetate by aldehyde dehydrogenase . (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"There is reduced fatty acid oxidation and lipoprotein secretion, leading to increased fatty acid uptake , which causes intracellular accumulation of triglycerides. (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The MEOS pathway activates CYP2E1, resulting in increased production of reactive oxygen species and more acetaldehyde, which is converted to acetate by aldehyde dehydrogenase . (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peroxisomal catalase protects cells from oxidative stress and maintains cellular integrity by neutralising effect."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcoholic liver disease is a reversible condition if timely intervention occurs and alcohol consumption is discontinued."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcohol liver disease is the most common condition leading to cirrhosis in India."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cytosolic ADH,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Microsomal ethanol oxidising system (MEOS)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peroxisomal catalase"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"There is reduced fatty acid oxidation and lipoprotein secretion, leading to increased fatty acid uptake , which causes intracellular accumulation of triglycerides. (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The MEOS pathway activates CYP2E1, resulting in increased production of reactive oxygen species and more acetaldehyde, which is converted to acetate by aldehyde dehydrogenase . (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Cirrhosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Micronodular Cirrhosis: Characterised by uniform nodules <3 mm in size. Commonly associated with: Alcoholic Liver Disease Chronic Biliary Obstruction and Cholangitis: Primary Biliary Cholangitis (PBC) Secondary Biliary Cholangitis Hemochromatosis Non-cirrhotic Portal Fibrosis (NCPF) 2. Macronodular Cirrhosis: Characterised by variable nodules >3 mm in size. Associated with: Viral Hepatitis: Hepatitis B Hepatitis C Hepatitis D Autoimmune Hepatitis ... (content truncated)"}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 60-year-old male with a history of alcohol use and chronic hepatitis presents with abdominal swelling, fatigue, difficulty eating, dark stools, and black vomitus. Examination shows splenomegaly, ascites and dilated veins around the umbilicus. Which of the following is the investigation of choice for this condition?","o":[{"t":"CT imaging"},{"t":"Blood counts"},{"t":"Endoscopy"},{"t":"Doppler ultrasound"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Doppler ultrasound"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The scenario describes a patient with chronic hepatitis and alcoholism presenting with splenomegaly, ascites, caput medusae (dilated veins around the umbilicus), hematemesis (dark vomitus), and hematochezia (dark stools)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is diagnosed as portal hypertension , with Doppler ultrasound being the investigation of choice."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations of Portal Hypertension:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigation Purpose/Findings"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ultrasonography Visualises features like splenomegaly and collateral"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"vessels; can identify causes like liver disease or portal vein thrombosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Doppler Ultrasound Investigation of choice to measure pressure gradient."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) > 5 mm Hg indicates portal hypertension."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Blood Counts (CBC) First biochemical sign is thrombocytopenia (due to (Option B ruled out) hypersplenism)."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It also shows leukopenia and anaemia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Endoscopy Used to check for gastro-oesophageal varices. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CT and MRI Scan (Option Assess the extent of portal vein thrombosis and A ruled out) evaluate hepatic vein patency."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"HVPG Measurement It is rarely performed by interventional radiology to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"measure hepatic venous pressure gradient directly."}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 58-year-old obese female with a history of DVT and recent appendectomy presents with worsening abdominal pain, swelling, fatigue, dark stools, and leg swelling. Examination reveals splenomegaly and findings, which are shown in the image below. Which of the following pathologies is most likely responsible for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Veno-occlusive disease"},{"t":"Portal vein thrombosis"},{"t":"Inferior vena caval webs"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Portal vein thrombosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The scenario presented, featuring a history of DVT and recent appendectomy, along with splenomegaly, dark stools (hematochezia), and caput medusae (above image), indicates a diagnosis of Portal Hypertension , most likely due to portal vein thrombosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Causes of Portal Hypertension"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pre-hepatic causes Hepatic causes Post-hepatic causes Portal vein thrombosis (OptionC) Pre-sinusoidal Budd Chiari Syndrome Splenic vein thrombosis Schistosomiasis Inferior vena cava webs Banti syndrome (characterised by Congenital hepatic fibrosis (Option D) massive splenomegaly of idiopathic Sinusoidal Restrictive cardiomyopat origin and is more commonly Cirrhosis hy observed in Japan). Alcoholic hepatitis Constrictive pericarditis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Post-sinusoidal (reduced compliance) Veno-occlusive disease (Option A) (hepatic sinusoidal Severe congestive heart obstruction) (Option B) failure Radiation Herbal tea Endemic ascites"},{"k":"img","v":9405}],"qi":[9328]},{"n":15,"q":"A 55-year-old male with a history of chronic alcohol use presents with worsening abdominal swelling, fatigue, dizziness, increased abdominal size, and loss of appetite. Examination shows a hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) of over 5 mm Hg. What is one of the earliest signs of this condition?","o":[{"t":"Puddle sign"},{"t":"Splenomegaly"},{"t":"Coffee ground vomitus"},{"t":"Caput medusae"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Splenomegaly"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s history of chronic alcohol use, along with symptoms like abdominal swelling and an HVPG over 5 mm Hg, suggests portal hypertension , which occurs when there is increased pressure within the portal venous system."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenomegaly is one of the earliest signs of portal hypertension , caused by congestion in the splenic vein due to increased portal pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical manifestations: Splenomegaly (earliest clinical sign) , leading to hypersplenism (Option B) Ascites (increased splenic vein pressure) Puddle sign is positive (When the examiner taps one side of the abdomen while the patient is upright, a wave of fluid can be felt on the opposite side, suggesting fluid accumulation). (Option A) Shifting dullness (When a patient lies supine, the examiner percusses for dullness to indicate fluid. Turning thepatient to one side causes the fluid to shift, making the dull area resonant, which confirmsfree fluid presence). Fluid thrill (Tapping one side of the abdomen while placing a hand onthe opposite side; if a wave of fluid is felt, it suggests free fluid in the cavity) Esophagealvarices (pressure of >12 mmHg) Coffee ground vomitus (coffee ground like black vomit dueto hematemesis) (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenomegaly (earliest clinical sign) , leading to hypersplenism (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascites (increased splenic vein pressure) Puddle sign is positive (When the examiner taps one side of the abdomen while the patient is upright, a wave of fluid can be felt on the opposite side, suggesting fluid accumulation). (Option A) Shifting dullness (When a patient lies supine, the examiner percusses for dullness to indicate fluid. Turning the patient to one side causes the fluid to shift, making the dull area resonant, which confirms free fluid presence). Fluid thrill (Tapping one side of the abdomen while placing a hand on the opposite side; if a wave of fluid is felt, it suggests free fluid in the cavity)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Puddle sign is positive (When the examiner taps one side of the abdomen while the patient is upright, a wave of fluid can be felt on the opposite side, suggesting fluid accumulation). (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shifting dullness (When a patient lies supine, the examiner percusses for dullness to indicate fluid. Turning the patient to one side causes the fluid to shift, making the dull area resonant, which confirms free fluid presence)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid thrill (Tapping one side of the abdomen while placing a hand on the opposite side; if a wave of fluid is felt, it suggests free fluid in the cavity)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Esophageal varices (pressure of >12 mmHg) Coffee ground vomitus (coffee ground like black vomit due to hematemesis) (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coffee ground vomitus (coffee ground like black vomit due to hematemesis) (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenomegaly (earliest clinical sign) , leading to hypersplenism (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascites (increased splenic vein pressure) Puddle sign is positive (When the examiner taps one side of the abdomen while the patient is upright, a wave of fluid can be felt on the opposite side, suggesting fluid accumulation). (Option A) Shifting dullness (When a patient lies supine, the examiner percusses for dullness to indicate fluid. Turning the patient to one side causes the fluid to shift, making the dull area resonant, which confirms free fluid presence). Fluid thrill (Tapping one side of the abdomen while placing a hand on the opposite side; if a wave of fluid is felt, it suggests free fluid in the cavity)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Puddle sign is positive (When the examiner taps one side of the abdomen while the patient is upright, a wave of fluid can be felt on the opposite side, suggesting fluid accumulation). (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shifting dullness (When a patient lies supine, the examiner percusses for dullness to indicate fluid. Turning the patient to one side causes the fluid to shift, making the dull area resonant, which confirms free fluid presence)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid thrill (Tapping one side of the abdomen while placing a hand on the opposite side; if a wave of fluid is felt, it suggests free fluid in the cavity)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Esophageal varices (pressure of >12 mmHg) Coffee ground vomitus (coffee ground like black vomit due to hematemesis) (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coffee ground vomitus (coffee ground like black vomit due to hematemesis) (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Puddle sign is positive (When the examiner taps one side of the abdomen while the patient is upright, a wave of fluid can be felt on the opposite side, suggesting fluid accumulation). (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shifting dullness (When a patient lies supine, the examiner percusses for dullness to indicate fluid. Turning the patient to one side causes the fluid to shift, making the dull area resonant, which confirms free fluid presence)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid thrill (Tapping one side of the abdomen while placing a hand on the opposite side; if a wave of fluid is felt, it suggests free fluid in the cavity)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coffee ground vomitus (coffee ground like black vomit due to hematemesis) (Option C)"},{"k":"img","v":9412},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Portal Hypertension:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Portal hypertension is defined as the elevation of the hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) to more than 5 mmHg."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mechanism: Increase in resistance of blood flow within the portal vein. Increased splanchnic blood (vasodilation in the splanchnic vascular bed).."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increase in resistance of blood flow within the portal vein."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased splanchnic blood (vasodilation in the splanchnic vascular bed).."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increase in resistance of blood flow within the portal vein."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased splanchnic blood (vasodilation in the splanchnic vascular bed).."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Black stools (hematozhezia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rectal haemorrhoids."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Portosystemic (portocaval) collaterals:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anastomosis between the portal and systemic venous systems creates routes of collateral circulation in cases of portal obstruction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These include: Site Tributaries of portal Tributaries of systemic Associated Clinical"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"vein vein Condition"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Esophagus (lower Left gastric vein Esophageal veins Esophageal varices end) (esophageal veins)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rectum (lower end) Superior rectal vein Middle and inferior rectal Rectal haemorrhoids"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"veins"},{"k":"img","v":9328},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Umbilicus Paraumbilical vein Above:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Superior epigastric vein Lateral thoracic vein Below: Superficial epigastric vein Inferior epigastric vein Sides: Posterior intercostal and lumbar vein"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Abdominal wall Splenic vein Left renal vein - (posterior)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bare area of liver Portal radicles Diaphragmatic vein Not seen with the naked"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"eye"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Falciform ligament Paraumbilical vein Diaphragmatic vein -"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ligamentum Left branch of portal Inferior vena cava Patent ductus venosus of venosum vein liver"}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 52-year-old male with chronic hepatitis presents with fatigue, mild abdominal discomfort, decreased appetite, and occasional jaundice over the past few months but denies significant weight loss or night sweats. A liver biopsy showed fibrosis with expansion of most portal zones and occasional bridging. What Metavir fibrosis stage does this indicate?","o":[{"t":"F1"},{"t":"F2"},{"t":"F3"},{"t":"F4"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) F2"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Metavir fibrosis scoring system categorises stage F2 as fibrosis characterised by the expansion of most portal zones and occasional bridging."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fibrosis extent in liver biopsies is usually assessed using the METAVIR or Ishak scales. METAVIR fibrosis scale"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"METAVIR score Stage of fibrosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"F0 No fibrosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"F1 (Option A) Portal zone fibrosis (few)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"F2 (Option B) Portal zone fibrosis and occasional (few) bridging."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"F3 (Option C) Portal zone fibrosis, marked bridging, and occasional"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"nodules"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"F4 (Option D) Cirrhosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ishak fibrosis scale"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ishak Grade Stage of fibrosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"0 No fibrosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"1 Some portal zone fibrosis with short fibrosis septa"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"2 Most portal zone fibrosis with short fibrosis septa"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"3 Most portal zone fibrosis with an occasional"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"portal-to-portal (P-P) bridging"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"4 Most portal zone fibrosis with marked portal-to-portal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(P-P) as well as portal-central (P-C) bridging"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"5 Marked portal-to-portal (P-P) and/or portal-central (P-C)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"bridging with occasional nodules (incomplete cirrhosis)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"6 Complete cirrhosis"}]},{"n":17,"q":"A 47-year-old woman with a history of non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) presents for routine follow-up. She reports mild fatigue but denies significant abdominal pain or jaundice. On examination, she has no stigmata of chronic liver disease. The physician wants to assess the degree of liver fibrosis. Which of the following is the best non-invasive test to evaluate liver stiffness?","o":[{"t":"Computed tomography"},{"t":"Abdominal ultrasound"},{"t":"Magnetic resonance imaging"},{"t":"Transient elastography"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Transient elastography"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Transient elastography, also called fibroscan , uses a specialised ultrasound device to measure liver stiffness by emitting a series of low-frequency vibrations (or shear waves) into the liver ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Non-invasive investigations for Cirrhosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Fibrotest This test assesses specific blood markers to determine"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cirrhosis, Blood markers: Bilirubin Gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) Haptoglobin Apolipoprotein A a2 - Macroglobulin,"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serum electrophoresis In chronic liver disease, this shows decreased albumin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and elevated g Globulin."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Live function test LFTs are commonly used for screening liver health."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"In chronic hepatitis, both ALT and AST are elevated, with ALT typically greater than AST (ALT > AST). These tests also evaluate vital parameters such as bilirubin and albumin to assess liver function."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Computed Tomography (Option A) can provide detailed images of liver morphology and help identify lesions; it does not measure liver stiffness directly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Its primary use is in detecting structural abnormalities rather than evaluating fibrosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ultrasound (Option B) is used as an initial investigation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It provides information on liver morphology and assesses complications like portal hypertension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can provide general information about liver conditions but lacks the specificity for assessing fibrosis levels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Magnetic resonance imaging (Option C) is useful for visualising liver anatomy and detecting lesions; however, like CT scans, it does not directly measure liver stiffness."}]},{"n":18,"q":"A 58-year-old female with a history of chronic hepatitis C and cirrhosis presents with increasing abdominal distension, jaundice, and fatigue. On examination, she has ascites and spider nevi. Laboratory tests reveal elevated bilirubin levels, low albumin, and elevated liver enzymes. All of the following are criteria for Child-Pugh score classification except?","o":[{"t":"Ascites"},{"t":"Albumin"},{"t":"Bilirubin"},{"t":"AST/ALT"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) AST/ALT"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The Child-Pugh classification is employed to assess the severity of liver disease, predict prognosis for liver cirrhosis, and determine a patient's eligibility for liver transplantation, using a scoring system that incorporates parameters such as:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"INR"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Albumin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascites"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic encephalopathy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It does not include liver enzymes like AST/ALT under the criteria."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Child-Pugh classification"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Parameters 1 2 3"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Encephalopathy None Grade 1-2 Grade 3-4"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Minimal) (Advanced)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ascites None Mildly controlled Poorly controlled"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bilirubin (mg/dL) < 2 2 - 3 > 3"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Albumin (g/dL) > 3.5 3 - 3.5 < 3.5"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"INR < 1.7 1.7 - 2.3 > 2.3"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Child-Turcotte-Pugh Class is determined by summing the scores for each parameter."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Category A : 5-6 (Compensated cirrhosis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Category B : 7-9 (Decompensated cirrhosis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Category C : > 10"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Child-Pugh score has inherent bias related to ascites and encephalopathy, potentially failing to reflect their severity accurately."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This limitation is reduced in the MELD scoring system."}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 62-year-old male with cirrhosis presents to the emergency department with upper gastrointestinal bleeding and a Child-Pugh score of 10. Which scoring system is most appropriate for assessing his mortality risk related to liver disease?","o":[{"t":"PELD score"},{"t":"Rockall risk score"},{"t":"Forrest classification system"},{"t":"MELD score"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) MELD score"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The Model for End-Stage Liver Disease (MELD) system assesses the severity of chronic liver disease and predicts survival , particularly in patients with cirrhosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The parameters are Serum bilirubin INR Serum creatinine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"INR"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum creatinine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recent modifications have been made to enhance its accuracy by adding new parameters to the existing ones, lik e Serum sodium Need for hemodialysis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum sodium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Need for hemodialysis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The range of the score is 6 to 40"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Liver transplant should be planned before the MELD score reaches 20, as mortality rates increase by 2.5 times beyond this threshold."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A higher score increases the chances of being listed for an orthotopic liver transplant."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"INR"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum creatinine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum sodium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Need for hemodialysis"},{"k":"img","v":9419},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pediatric End-Stage Liver Disease (PELD) score is used for children . The parameters used are (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"INR"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Albumin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Growth failure"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rockall risk score (Option B) determines the severity and predicts mortality in patients with upper gastrointestinal bleeding. Parameters are"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heart rate"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood pressure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Comorbidities"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endoscopic findings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"History of recent haemorrhage"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Forest classification system (Option C) can be used to describe the appearance of the ulcer and the risk of bleeding or rebleeding in patients with peptic ulcers ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Class Ia Spurting haemorrhage"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Class Ib Oozing haemorrhage"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Class IIa Non-bleeding visible vessel"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Class IIb Adherent clot"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Class IIc Flat pigmented spot"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Class III Clean ulcer base"}]},{"n":20,"q":"A 68-year-old man with a history of cirrhosis presents with increased fatigue, and examination reveals multiple vascular lesions on the skin. His liver function tests show significant elevations in AST and ALT. What is the primary pathophysiological mechanism responsible for the formation of these lesions in cirrhosis?","o":[{"t":"Due to engorged superficial veins"},{"t":"Chronic Hypoxia"},{"t":"Because of platelet-derived growth factor"},{"t":"Increased vascularity due to estrogen"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Increased vascularity due to estrogen"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The image depicts spider nevi , vascular lesions with a central arteriole and radiating small vessels, commonly seen in cirrhosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired liver function reduces estrogen metabolism, leading to increased estrogen levels, vasodilation, and increased vascularity, which form small, dilated blood vessels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spider nevi typically appear on the skin's surface in areas the superior vena cava supplies, such as the face, neck, and upper body."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They can also be observed in conditions like pregnancy and rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical signs & symptoms of Cirrhosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Vague symptoms Abdominal pain (in the right upper quadrant)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Fever Nausea Vomiting Diarrhea Anorexia"},{"k":"img","v":9426},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Palmar erythema"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It is characterised by reddening of the palms due to increased estrogen levels from impaired liver function, which leads to vasodilation and increased blood flow and serves as an important clinical indicator of liver dysfunction."},{"k":"img","v":9431},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dupuytren's contracture"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It happens due to thickening and shortening of the palmar fascia. It can start on the ring finger and involve the little finger."},{"k":"img","v":9436},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clubbing"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) can be released, leading to tissue proliferation at the base of the nail."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Option C) This results in a bulbous appearance, and it is not very specific as can be seen in other conditions."},{"k":"img","v":9442},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Parotid enlargement"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Testicular atrophy This can result from a combination of increased"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"estrogen, decreased testosterone production and altered hormonal regulation Other manifestations in male patients: Gynecomastia, Decreased body hair Muscle wasting."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Jaundice This occurs due to the liver's impaired ability to process"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and excrete bilirubin , resulting in its accumulation in the bloodstream."},{"k":"img","v":9444},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Kayser–Fleischer rings"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Seen in cirrhosis caused by Wilson’s disease, they are greenish-brown deposits of coppe r that form in the cornea due to affected copper metabolism."},{"k":"img","v":9447},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Asterixis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":9452},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Caput medusae"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It is the appearance of engorged superficial veins on the abdominal wall and is a clinical sign of portal hypertension often seen in cirrhosis. (Option A) ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic hypoxia (Option B) can lead to various systemic changes and conditions like clubbing or cyanosis, but it does not typically cause spider naevi, which is associated with hormonal changes."}],"qi":[9337]},{"n":21,"q":"A 72-year-old male with chronic hepatitis C presents with severe fatigue, confusion, and increasing abdominal girth. Examination reveals jaundice, asterixis, and bilateral oedema. Laboratory tests show elevated bilirubin, transaminases, prolonged prothrombin time, and thrombocytopenia. An endoscopy shows the presence of non-bleeding varices. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Compensated cirrhosis"},{"t":"Alcoholic hepatitis"},{"t":"Acute liver failure"},{"t":"Decompensated cirrhosis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Decompensated cirrhosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Based on the clinical scenario provided, the most likely diagnosis is decompensated cirrhosis with portal hypertension, leading to the development of non-bleeding varices."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decompensated cirrhosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients developing complications due to cirrhosis are considered to have decompensated cirrhosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prognosis of patients with this condition is poor ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complications of cirrhosis: Ascites, variceal bleed, jaundice Splenomegaly Portal HTN Coagulopathy Bacterial peritonitis Hepatic encephalopathy Hepatorenal syndrome Hepatopulmonary syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascites, variceal bleed, jaundice"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Portal HTN"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coagulopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bacterial peritonitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic encephalopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatorenal syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatopulmonary syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascites, variceal bleed, jaundice"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Portal HTN"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coagulopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bacterial peritonitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatic encephalopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatorenal syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatopulmonary syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Compensated cirrhosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is the early stage of cirrhosis, where the liver is significantly damaged yet still performs most essential functions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients often remain asymptomatic or experience only mild symptoms due to the liver's compensatory mechanisms. Type of cirrhosis Ascites Varices Death by 1 year"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Compensated Stage 1 No No 1% cirrhosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Stage 2 No Yes 3%"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Decompensated Stage 3 Yes Yes 20 % cirrhosis Stage 4 Yes Yes (+/- Bleeding) 57%"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alcoholic hepatitis (Option B) is an inflammatory liver condition caused by excessive alcohol consumption , resulting in jaundice, abdominal pain, and liver dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While it can lead to cirrhosis, the patient's chronic hepatitis C history is more consistent with decompensated cirrhosis based on the current clinical presentation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute liver failure (Option C) is a rapid decline in liver function over days or weeks, usually in individuals without pre-existing liver diseas e."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can result from acetaminophen overdose, viral hepatitis, drug-induced liver injury, or metabolic disorders."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, the chronic history of hepatitis C does not align with an acute presentation."}]}]);
